{
  "summary": "Candida albicans is a common fungus that can cause painful oral infections, particularly in people with weakened immune systems. To fight this infection, the body relies on white blood cells called neutrophils. This study used a mouse model to investigate how the body coordinates this immune response. Researchers discovered that a specific signaling pathway, the interleukin-1 receptor (IL-1R), is essential for controlling the fungus at the start of an infection.

The study found that IL-1R signaling works through two distinct channels. First, it triggers keratinocytes (the primary cells in the tongue’s surface) to produce chemical signals that recruit existing neutrophils from the blood to the site of infection. Second, it prompts endothelial cells (which line blood vessels) to release a growth factor called G-CSF. This factor travels to the bone marrow to stimulate the production of new neutrophils, ensuring a steady supply of "emergency" immune cells to meet the high demand caused by the infection.

Interestingly, the researchers identified that keratinocytes act as the first responders; they release a protein called IL-1α upon contact with the fungus, which then alerts the nearby blood vessels to start the defense process. These findings reveal a vital communication link between different cell types in the mouth that is necessary to prevent the fungus from spreading and causing severe disease."
}
